{
  "dom_id": "step-q250075",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+5x-1$ and the line $y=-2x-13$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+5x-1 \\\\ y=-2x-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+5x-1 & =-2x-13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=-3.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-2{x}_{1}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-4)-13 \\\\  & =-5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,-5).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =-2{x}_{2}-13 \\\\  & =-2(-3)-13 \\\\  & =-7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(-3,-7).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227274/assets/782a5e0fbe4c362ca17e86a11f017f6909873cb1f06f0d801d6d958bd1c8ab44.png)",
